{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS002738","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"All four candidates are direct matches to the target concept via alcohol dependence/alcohol use disorder: PGS002738 and PGS002739 have trait_efo="alcohol dependence" and phenotyping_reported="Alcohol use disorder"; PGS000201 and PGS000202 are labeled "Problematic alcohol use" but their phenotyping_reported is still AUD (DSM-5 criteria count, log-transformed), supporting endpoint alignment.

PGS002738 is best supported because it matches the target EFO exactly ("alcohol dependence"), uses a modern genome-wide shrinkage method (PRS-CS) with a substantial variant count (326,000), is developed from large disease-focused cohorts (MVP, UKB), and reports a reasonably strong discrimination-relevant effect metric (OR top 10% vs rest = 1.96, 95% CI 1.54–2.51) in a EUR evaluation sample (n=7,900).

Main limitation: across all candidates, there is no clearly PRS-only AUC/R2 reported, and PGS002738 has covariates listed as "Unknown," reducing comparability and preventing a high-confidence, clearly superior choice versus PGS000201 (which reports ΔR2=0.01192 with explicit basic covariates and ~1.09M variants) or PGS002739 (strong OR top 10% but evaluated in African American ancestry rather than EUR)."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS002738","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"All four candidates are direct matches to the target concept (alcohol dependence/AUD spectrum): PGS002738 and PGS002739 have trait_efo="alcohol dependence" with phenotyping_reported="Alcohol use disorder"; PGS000201/PGS000202 are labeled "Problematic alcohol use" but their phenotyping_reported is also AUD DSM-5 criteria count (log-transformed), aligning reasonably with alcohol dependence risk.

Among candidates, PGS002738 has the strongest visible discriminative evidence for a direct alcohol dependence endpoint: OR(top 10% vs rest)=1.96 (CI 1.54–2.51) in a European evaluation sample (n=7,900), which is more interpretable for case-control risk stratification than the small incremental ΔR² values reported for PGS000201 (0.01192) and PGS000202 (0.00456). PGS002738 also comes from a disease-focused AUD risk evaluation context (PRS + family history study), while not appearing to be a pan-trait framework score.

Main limitation: metrics are not fully comparable across models (PGS002738 reports an extreme-tail OR rather than PRS-only R²/AUC; covariates are "Unknown" for both PGS002738 and PGS002739; PGS000201/PGS000202 report ΔR² but not PRS-only R²/AUC). Additionally, PGS002739 is evaluated in African American cohorts (AFR) and is not directly comparable if the intended deployment ancestry is European/unspecified. These gaps prevent a high-confidence, clearly best-supported selection."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS001280","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"All 7 candidates are direct matches to pulmonary embolism, but they fall into two main groups: (1) four pan-trait UK Biobank framework scores from the “Significant sparse polygenic risk scores across 813 traits…” paper (PGS001277–PGS001280), and (2) three non-framework PE scores (PGS003861 EAS-only; PGS004460/PGS004530 EUR-only, risk-factor integration paper). Per the provided domain knowledge, thromboembolic targets should generally prefer a coherent cluster with basic covariates and explicit PRS-only metrics over a lone small single-ancestry high full-model AUROC, and full-model AUROC should not be substituted for PRS-only performance.

Within the UKB framework cluster, PGS001280 is the cleanest phenotype alignment to the target (trait_efo and phenotyping_reported are pulmonary-embolism-specific: “PE (time-to-event)” / “TTE PE”), and it has strong, internally consistent PRS-comparable metrics reported (PGS-only AUROC=0.60765; PGS-only R2=0.01403) with basic covariates (age, sex, array, PCs). Its full-model AUROC (0.67617) is similar to siblings, and its PRS-only R2 remains below the local heritability ceiling signal provided (sanity-check passes). Compared to other siblings, it also avoids explicit DVT composite wording.

However, confidence is limited because the selected model is still from a pan-trait framework (a known generalization risk per the domain knowledge), and the alternative non-framework EUR I26 models (PGS004460/PGS004530) have genome-wide variant counts but provide no PRS-comparable metrics (only an OR without clear per-SD definition) and unknown covariates, making a decisive superiority call impossible from the visible evidence."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_HIGH_QUALITY","best_model_id":"PGS000471","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"All three candidates (PGS000469/470/471) are direct matches: trait_reported, trait_efo, and phenotyping_reported are all 'Carcinoma in situ of skin', aligning exactly to the target 'skin carcinoma in situ'. They share the same publication family, EUR-only GWAS/DEV/EVAL, same training cohort (MGI), same validation size (n=5,500), and similar/basic covariates (age, sex, batch, PCs 1-4), so differences are primarily performance and (secondarily) model structure.

Among them, PGS000471 (lassosum; 7 variants) has the strongest visible validation metrics within the same endpoint/covariate context: highest full-model AUROC (0.569 vs 0.557 vs 0.524), highest Nagelkerke pseudo-R² (0.0255 vs 0.0154 vs 0.00141), and strongest effect size per SD (OR 1.401 [1.297–1.513] vs 1.308 vs 1.09). With no PGS-only AUROC/R² reported for any model, these within-family, like-for-like comparisons are the most consistent basis to pick a best candidate.

Main limitation: the reported discrimination metrics are full-model (covariate-adjusted) rather than PRS-only, and variant counts are very small for PGS000470/471, which makes standalone PRS contribution harder to infer; therefore confidence is moderate rather than high."}
